        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Searching for a treadmill for sale returns a single undifferentiated list of prices, and
          that list hides the thing that decides whether the purchase goes well. A treadmill is not
          one market. It is four, they carry different prices for the same machine, and they differ
          far more in what happens after the sale than in what happens at checkout.
        </p>

        <div id="four-markets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Four Markets Selling You the Same Machine</h2>
<p>
            Almost every treadmill listing you will see falls into one of four categories, and the
            same model can appear in all four at once at four different prices. The gap between
            the cheapest and the most expensive is routinely 40%, and almost none of that gap is
            the hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>New, at full retail.</strong> Manufacturer direct or a national retailer,
            current model year, full warranty, and the price the brand would like to hold. Outside
            the discount windows this is what you pay, and the compensations are a complete
            warranty, a return path that exists on paper, and delivery options that go beyond the
            kerb.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Model-year clearance.</strong> The outgoing version of a current machine, sold
            off to clear floor and warehouse space. This is usually the best value in the whole
            category, because annual refreshes in treadmills are overwhelmingly console and
            software changes over an unchanged frame, motor and deck. The warranty is normally
            identical to the current model's.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Open-box and factory refurbished.</strong> Returned, inspected and resold,
            typically 15-30% below new. The variable is not condition — it is warranty. Some
            refurbished units carry the full original term, some carry 90 days, and the listing
            frequently does not say which. That single question is worth more than the discount.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Private used.</strong> Marketplace listings, estate sales, gym liquidations.
            This is where the money is, and where all the risk is: no warranty, no return, and no
            way to know what the previous owner did or did not do to the belt.
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">New versus used</a>
            works through the trade properly, and the
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used buying checklist</a>
            is what to run before handing anybody cash.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-pay" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What to Pay: The Bands and Where They Break</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill quality does not rise smoothly with price. It steps, and knowing where the
            steps fall is what stops you paying $1,400 for a $900 machine with a bigger screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Under $500.</strong> Genuinely constrained rather than merely cheap. Motors sit
            below 2.5 CHP, decks are short, and the frame is light enough to move under a jogger.
            For walking this can be entirely adequate, and at this budget a walking pad is often
            the better product than a compromised full-size machine —
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the under-$500 guide</a>
            covers which is which.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>$800 to $1,500.</strong> The band where the money starts buying hardware
            instead of features, and also the band with the widest spread between the best and
            worst machines at an identical price. Two treadmills at $1,200 can differ by a full
            horsepower and five inches of deck. See
            <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under $1,000</a>
            and <a href="/treadmills-under-1500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under $1,500</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>$1,500 to $2,500.</strong> Where 60-inch decks meet frames heavy enough to run
            on. If you intend to run rather than walk, this is the first band that reliably
            delivers it, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running guide</a>
            explains why deck length outranks every other specification here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Above $2,500.</strong> Real improvements that compound slowly — AC motors,
            heavier rollers, warranty terms written for daily use.
            <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">The premium tier</a>
            and <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial machines</a>
            cover what you will actually notice. If you are unsure which band you belong in, start
            with <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much you should spend</a>
            rather than with a listing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-prices-fall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. When the Price Actually Falls</h2>
<p>
            The same machine swings 20-35% across a calendar year, and the swing is predictable
            because it is driven by inventory rather than by demand. Treadmills are physically
            large, warehousing them is expensive, and the model-year changeover is the point at
            which holding last season's stock stops being worth it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two windows matter. The first is the late-autumn promotional period, where the
            discounting is real but the stock is current-year and the reductions are calculated to
            look larger than they are. The second is the model-year changeover, where the outgoing
            unit is cleared at a genuine loss — smaller headline percentages, larger real savings,
            and far less selection.
          </p>
          <p>
            The window to avoid is January. Demand peaks, inventory is thin, and prices are
            frequently higher than they were six weeks earlier while the marketing says the
            opposite. Our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">timing guide</a>
            has the full cycle month by month.
          </p>
          <p>
            One practical habit is worth more than any calendar: record the price you see today,
            with the date, for the two or three machines on your shortlist. A "was $2,299, now
            $1,599" banner is only information if you know what the machine actually sold for last
            month, and in this category the reference price is very often a number the machine has
            not been sold at in a year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Where to Buy, and What Each Seller Is Good At</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Manufacturer direct.</strong> The most reliable warranty path, because there is
            nobody to route a claim through, and usually the best delivery options — including
            in-room placement and assembly as a paid add-on. Rarely the cheapest outside a
            promotion, and the return policy is normally the most restrictive of any channel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>National retailers and big-box.</strong> Competitive pricing, frequent
            promotions, and the most forgiving returns — which matters more than it sounds, because
            a treadmill is the hardest consumer product to send back. Delivery is usually
            kerbside unless you pay more, and floor staff are not a reliable source of
            specification detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Amazon and large marketplaces.</strong> Broad selection and honest reviews at
            volume, but the listing is the weak point: model names repeat across model years, and
            the specification block is frequently the previous generation's. Confirm the deck
            dimensions, motor rating and warranty against the manufacturer's own page before
            ordering, not after.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Specialty fitness dealers.</strong> The channel people forget. Prices are close
            to retail, but you get a machine you can stand on before buying, a local service
            relationship, and delivery that puts the treadmill where you want it. For anything
            above roughly $2,000, or for a buyer who cannot handle a 300-pound box, that is worth
            paying for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Private used listings.</strong> Roughly twice the hardware per dollar, zero
            recourse. Viable if you inspect properly and can move the machine, and a bad idea
            sight-unseen at any price. Check the
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan and repair arithmetic</a>
            before you decide what a ten-year-old machine is worth to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Delivery, Assembly and the Cost of Changing Your Mind</h2>
<p>
            The advertised price is not the cost. A full-size treadmill arrives as a single crated
            item between roughly 200 and 350 pounds, and what happens to that crate is a separate
            transaction from buying the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Kerbside is the default.</strong> The pallet is left at the end of your
            driveway and the rest is yours. Getting it up a flight of stairs takes two capable
            people and a strap, and this is the most common point at which a purchase becomes a
            problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Threshold, in-room and assembled are three different services.</strong> They
            are priced separately and named inconsistently between sellers. In-room placement plus
            assembly typically runs $150-$300 and is the single best money you can spend if you
            live above the ground floor or are doing this alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Returns are the real asymmetry.</strong> A treadmill you dislike is not a
            product you send back casually — restocking fees, return shipping on a freight item,
            and the requirement that it be re-crated all apply, and the combined cost can approach
            a third of the purchase price. This is precisely why a generous return policy is worth
            more than a 10% discount, and why standing on the machine at a dealer first is not an
            old-fashioned idea.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And the room.</strong> Measure the space including the clearance behind the
            deck before you order anything, because a machine that fits the floor plan but not the
            run-off area is in the wrong room.
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Dimensions and space requirements</a>
            gives the numbers, and
            <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding mechanisms</a>
            explains what a folding machine does and does not save you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-market" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Buying Used Without Buying Someone Else’s Problem</h2>
<p>
            The used market is the only place a $2,500 machine becomes a $900 one, and the
            discount is real because the sellers are motivated: a treadmill that is not being used
            is furniture nobody wants, and moving it is expensive. That asymmetry is in your
            favour, and it is also why so many listings are optimistic.
          </p>
          <p>
            Run it under power before money changes hands. A machine that cannot be demonstrated
            is a machine with something to hide, and "it just needs a belt" is the most expensive
            sentence in this market — a controller board failure presents exactly the same way to
            somebody who has not checked properly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walk on it at 3 mph and then at 6. Listen for a squeal that changes with speed, feel
            for hesitation as your foot lands, and watch whether the belt drifts toward one rail.
            Then raise the incline through its full range and lower it again. Those four checks
            catch most of what is expensive:
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slipping</a>,
            <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">bearing and roller noise</a>,
            and a motor that is struggling under load.
          </p>
          <p>
            Budget for immediate maintenance regardless of how it presents. A used machine of
            unknown history should get a deck lubrication and a belt tension check the week it
            arrives, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance checklist</a>
            is the ten minutes that decides whether you got a bargain. Assume the previous owner
            did none of it, because statistically they did not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="red-flags" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Seven Listings to Walk Away From</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A specification block that does not match the manufacturer's.</strong> Usually
            a previous model year copied forward. Sometimes a different machine entirely under a
            similar name.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peak horsepower quoted without a continuous-duty rating.</strong> Peak is a
            burst figure the motor cannot sustain and exists for the box. If the seller will not
            give you a CHP number, assume the reason is that it is low — our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            covers the distinction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A "was" price with no history.</strong> Discounts in this category are measured
            against reference prices that are frequently fictional. Compare to what competitors
            charge for the same specification, never to the seller's own crossed-out number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Refurbished with an unstated warranty.</strong> Ask for the term in writing
            before buying. A 90-day warranty on a refurbished treadmill is a different product from
            a full-term one at the same discount.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery described only as "shipping included."</strong> Included to where?
            Kerbside and in-room differ by a flight of stairs and a few hundred dollars.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A used machine that cannot be run.</strong> No power at the property, no cord,
            "already disassembled for moving." Walk away, every time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A subscription you have not priced.</strong> Some consoles do very little
            without an active membership, and over five years that fee can exceed the machine.
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            sets out what each answer costs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time to buy a treadmill on sale?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The late-autumn promotional period and the model-year changeover are the two windows where discounts are real. January is the worst time — demand peaks, stock thins, and prices are often higher than they were six weeks earlier. The outgoing model year is usually the best value in the category, because annual refreshes are overwhelmingly console changes over unchanged hardware.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to buy a treadmill online or in a store?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Online is usually cheaper at checkout and a specialty dealer is often cheaper by the time the machine is running in your house. The dealer price typically includes delivery into the room and assembly, which online sellers charge $150-$300 for as an add-on, and you get to stand on the machine before committing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are refurbished treadmills worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Frequently yes, at 15-30% below new — but the discount is not the variable that matters. Warranty term is. Factory-refurbished units range from a full original warranty to 90 days, listings often do not say which, and a refurbished machine with 90 days of cover is a materially different purchase from one with the full term.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I pay for a used treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As a rough anchor, expect to pay 40-50% of current street price for a well-maintained machine three to five years old, and considerably less beyond that. Only pay anything at all for a machine you have run under power and walked on at two speeds. Budget for a lubrication and belt tension check on arrival regardless of what the seller says.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does delivery of a treadmill actually involve?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A crated item of roughly 200-350 pounds delivered to the kerb unless you pay for more. Threshold, in-room and in-room-plus-assembly are three separately priced services named inconsistently between sellers. If you live above the ground floor or will be handling it alone, paying for in-room assembly is the best-value line on the invoice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I return a treadmill if I do not like it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Technically usually, practically rarely. Restocking fees, freight return shipping and a requirement to re-crate the machine can add up to a third of the purchase price. That asymmetry is the reason a forgiving return policy is worth more than a small discount, and the reason trying a machine in person is still the best risk control available.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill on Sale</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these costs money at a different stage — at checkout, on delivery day, or in year two when the warranty claim is refused.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping the discount instead of the specification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 40% reduction on an over-priced machine is still an over-priced machine. Fix the specification you need first — deck length, continuous-duty rating, weight capacity — and only then compare what the market is charging for it. The best-value purchase in this category is almost always an outgoing model year at a modest discount, not a heavily discounted machine that was never worth the reference price.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not asking what the warranty covers on a refurbished or clearance unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Warranty is the single largest hidden variable in a discounted treadmill. Terms on refurbished stock range from the full original period to 90 days, clearance units occasionally carry the outgoing term rather than the current one, and almost no listing states it plainly. Ask in writing before you pay, and treat an evasive answer as the answer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating delivery as a formality</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill is a freight item, and kerbside delivery means exactly that. People discover this while standing in a driveway beside a pallet at dusk. Decide before ordering whether you are paying for in-room placement, and if you are not, arrange two people and a strap for the day it lands.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used machine you have not run under power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Every expensive fault in a used treadmill — a failing controller board, a worn deck, a motor struggling under load — presents as something cheap to a buyer who has not run the machine. Five minutes at 3 mph and 6 mph with the incline cycled through its range separates a bargain from a very heavy disposal problem.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

